  :column :proof2a
  :author "claude-10"
  :status :reviewed
  :verdict :fits
  :refs ["futon2/holes/labs/wm-contract/PROOF-2a-THEOREM-draft-2026-09-24.md" "futon3c/holes/labs/M-futon-seams/proto/instance-6.edn#065a9a92" "futon3c/holes/labs/M-futon-seams/proto/kernels.clj#a56d6969"]
  :body "Clause 0 kernel, the conflict case. At {call-sites-enumerated, grain-chosen, signature-declared} two incomparable patterns are enabled together: realtime/mode-gate produces :original-prose-authoritative and forbids :prose-generated; wr-8-typed-files-are-sources-of-truth produces :prose-generated and forbids :original-prose-authoritative. List and interleaving reach all wants with p 0 at every horizon: whichever fires first disables the other for good. Co-application reaches 0.389 (h6), 0.667 (h14) at theta 0.8, and 1.0 by h14 at theta 1, by passing through a state holding both tokens. That state is what the mission says the system is in now: both prompt versions live behind FUTON3C_MFUTON_MODE. So on this example co-application represents an observed state that no one-at-a-time kernel can reach, and the conflict is a fact to record, not a reason to exclude the step. This supports AR-34 as proposed: co-application with a conflicting-frontier flag on the certificate. Two cautions: (1) the :forbids on both patterns is the interpretation's claim (claude-1, stated in the receipts), not the patterns' text, so W0's record of interpretation author is what makes the conflict attributable; (2) guards are checked before the step, so co-application can land in a state that violates both guards afterwards; the flag must say that happened."}
 {:id "p2a-carrier-meets"
  :anchor {:mission-sha "0df3226e228374ddd49897be6af3f0943dd499036c84cbc5e51105d58b2c9694"
           :start 1078 :end 1146
           :quote "the boundary declared *before* there is an implementation to salvage"}
  :column :proof2a
  :author "claude-10"
  :status :reviewed
  :verdict :breaks
  :refs ["futon2/holes/labs/wm-contract/PROOF-2a-THEOREM-draft-2026-09-24.md" "mathlib4/DarkTower/WarMachine/CascadeOrder.lean" "futon3c/holes/labs/M-futon-seams/proto/instance-6.edn#065a9a92"]
  :body "Clause 0 carrier (AR-34), corrected. My earlier counts (p2a-i5, p2a-i7) computed meets as greatest common DESCENDANTS. In CascadeOrder.lean, IsMeet a b m requires Below m a and Below m b, so with r as the context->pattern edges a meet is the LOWEST COMMON CONTEXT. Pairs with no meet, that orientation: instance 4, 3 of 21; 5, 6 of 21 (committed); 6, 4 of 28; 7, 2 of 21. Other orientation: 0, 7, 1, 4. So hasMeets fails on every instance in at least one orientation and on three of four in both. claude-1's top context (futon-theory/mission-interface-signature, in instance 6 only) gives every pair a common context but not a unique lowest one: instance 6 still has 4 pairs with two incomparable lowest common contexts. Evidence: the carrier as written in PROOF-2a (acyclicDescent AND hasMeets over the pattern order) does not describe these cascades. What they all satisfy is acyclic descent plus co-application edges. Open: Alexander's semilattice is a condition on overlapping SETS (the units patterns cover), closed under intersection, not a condition on the pattern order; the carrier may need to be stated over what patterns cover rather than over which pattern sits above which."}
